.TH ODIN-SEAT 1 2026-10-15 "Odin Seat 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
odin-seat \- terminal multiplexer
with overlapping windows and an Alt-tap menu
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B odin-seat
.RB [ \-Dv ]
.RB [ \-\-3d\-focus=\fIpixels\fP ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-S
.IR path ]
.RI [ command
.RI [ argument
\&.\|.\|.]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B odin-seat
keeps a desktop of overlapping terminal windows in a server process and
paints it into the terminal you run it from.
Each window is a terminal emulator of its own whose screen, cursor and
modes live in the server, so a session survives detaching and every
program in it keeps running.
Run
.B odin-seat
with no command to attach to the default session, creating it with one
window if it does not exist.
.PP
Windows overlap on a stippled backdrop, each in a frame with its number
and title.
Opening a window places it on top; selecting one raises it.
The desktop is composed on every change and each attached terminal is
sent only the cells that differ from what it last saw, inside a
synchronized update, so redraws never tear.
.PP
The
.B menu key
is an isolated tap of Alt: press Alt and let go with nothing in between.
Alt held with a letter stays a chord for the focused window.
The menu pops up; a letter then acts, without waiting for the menu to be
read.
.B Ctrl\-\e
is the same key for a terminal that swallows the tap.
See
.B THE MENU KEY
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-D
Debug: a server started by this client stays in the foreground of its
own process group with logging on its standard error, and the client
logs too.
.TP
.B \-m
Paint this terminal in strict black and white, for an e-paper display:
every cell is ink on paper in the terminal's default colours, or paper on
ink (reverse video) where it had a coloured background or asked for
reverse video; shaded areas (the desktop backdrop, dim shade blocks) are
plain paper; dim, blink and underline colours are dropped, and no cursor
colour is sent.
Only this client is affected; another terminal attached to the same
session keeps its colours.
.B ODIN_SEAT_MONO=1
in the environment does the same.
.TP
.BI \-\-3d\-focus= pixels
On a terminal that draws Silkie stereo depth (it listed
.BR depth.1 ),
show the focused window, its frame and everything in it, text and images,
.I pixels
reference pixels nearer than the rest of the desktop; a negative number
puts it farther away.
The program's own depths inside the window move with it.
A depth given in millidegrees moves by the pixels at Silkie's default
52-degree field of view.
Moving focus repaints this terminal, and its images are placed again at
their new depth without sending their pixels.
Only this client is affected.
Silkie shows content in front of the screen only when its
.B spatial_negative_divergence
setting allows it, which it does not by default.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose logging on standard error.
.TP
.BI \-L " name"
Use the socket called
.I name
(the way
.BR tmux (1)
does); the default is
.BR default .
Two servers with different names are independent.
.TP
.BI \-S " path"
Use the socket at
.IR path .
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.PP
The global -A option explicitly authorizes the attached client's local
SSH_AUTH_SOCK as a fallback when the terminal has no provider. For example:
.nf
odin-seat -A attach
.fi
The native terminal provider is preferred. This lets a fresh SSH-forwarded
agent follow the attaching client, without leaving the session's applications
bound to a stale SSH login socket.
.SH COMMANDS
With no command,
.B odin-seat
attaches, creating a session if none exists.
Where a command takes
.BI \-s " name"
it acts on that session; otherwise on the default one.
Everything after
.B \-\-
is the program to run in a new window; the default is your shell.
.TP
.BI "attach [\-s " name ]
Attach this terminal to a session.
Several terminals may attach at once.
The desktop then has the rows and the columns of the smallest of them in
each dimension, as in
.BR tmux (1),
so each shows all of it; a terminal that leaves gives its rows and columns
back.
What a terminal can show is the most any of them can: programs are told
the cell with the most pixels, the widest gamut and the deepest display,
and each terminal is sent pictures and colours scaled or converted to its
own.
.TP
.BI "new-session [\-d] [\-x " cols "] [\-y " rows "] [\-s " name "] [\-\- " program " .\|.\|.]"
Create a session with one window.
.B \-d
creates it detached, sized
.IR cols x rows
(defaults 80x24), instead of attaching.
.TP
.BI "new-window [\-s " name "] [\-\- " program " .\|.\|.]"
Open another window in a session.
Run from inside a window it acts on the desktop you are in.
.TP
.BI "split-window [\-h|\-v] [\-s " name "] [\-w " N "] [\-\- " program " .\|.\|.]"
Divide the selected window and open a new program in the other half.
.B \-h
opens on the right (the default);
.B \-v
opens below.
The default target is the focused window.
Other windows keep their geometry, and both halves remain independently
movable windows. A zoomed window splits across the whole desktop.
If two frames and a content cell in each cannot fit, the command fails
without changing the original window.
.TP
.BI "kill-window [\-f] [\-s " name "] [\-w " N ]
Close the selected window, normally sending a hangup to its program.
.B \-f
instead sends SIGKILL to the foreground process group, the shell's process
group and the shell before closing the window. It requires no confirmation;
unsaved work in those programs is lost.
.TP
.BI "list-windows [\-s " name ]
One line per window: session, number, program, size, position,
stacking order and focus.
.TP
.B list-sessions
One line per session with its desktop size.
.TP
.B list-clients
One line per attached terminal: session, size, TERM, the terminal's own
name as reported by XTVERSION, and what it can do (kitty graphics,
sixel, cell pixel sizes).
.TP
.BI "agent-socket [\-s " name ]
Print the session's stable SSH_AUTH_SOCK pathname. New windows receive it
automatically. In an older shell, run:
.nf
export SSH_AUTH_SOCK="$(odin-seat agent-socket)"
.fi
Agent requests follow the most recently attached display; existing connections
fail when the provider changes, and new connections use its keys. See
.BR odin-seat-protocols (7).
.TP
.BI "kill-session [" name ]
End a session and every program in it.
.TP
.B kill-server
End every session and stop the server.
.TP
.B refresh
Repaint this terminal from scratch.
.TP
.BI "menu [\-s " name "] " key " [5]"
Press a menu key from a script, as if the menu key had been tapped and
.I key
typed:
a menu letter
.RB ( o ", " c ", " e ", " k ", " z ", " r ", " f ", " m ", " t ", " h ", " s ", " b ", " u ", " i ", " d ", " q ", " ? ", " 1 \- 9 ),
or one of
.BR menu ", " esc ", " enter ", " tab ", " backspace ", " up ", " down ", " left ", " right .
In copy mode or the switch list every key goes to that mode, so a script
can search, select and copy.
A second argument of
.B 5
moves five cells when placing a window.
.TP
.BI "send-keys [\-s " name "] [\-w " N "] [\-l] " text " .\|.\|."
Type
.I text
into a window (the focused one unless
.B \-w
names it), the arguments joined by spaces.
C escapes are decoded
.RB ( \en ", " \er ", " \et ", " \ee ", " \ex\fIHH\fR ", " \e\e )
unless
.B \-l
sends the text literally.
.TP
.BI "capture-window [\-s " name "] [\-w " N "] [\-S " lines ]
Print the window's text, one line per row, trailing blanks trimmed.
.B \-S
prepends that many scrollback lines
.RB ( \-S\ \- :
all of them).
.TP
.BI "pipe-window [\-s " name "] [\-w " N "] [" path ]
Append the window's raw output to
.I path
from now on; without a path, stop.
.TP
.BI "save [\-f " file ]
Write every session's windows to
.I file
(default
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/odin-seat/sessions ,
or
.IR ~/.local/state/odin-seat/sessions ):
place, size, title, flags, stacking, the directory each program is in
now, and the command it was started with.
.TP
.BI "restore [\-f " file ]
Recreate the sessions in the file, starting each window's command afresh
in its saved directory; a session that already exists is left alone.
.TP
.BI "upgrade [" binary ]
Replace the running server, in place, with
.I binary
(by default the
.B odin-seat
running this command): the server writes its state, hands its
descriptors to the new program and executes it in the same process, so
every program in every window and every attached terminal carries on.
Screens, scrollback, modes, keyboard flags, colours and layout survive;
images are dropped until the program draws them again.
A client that finds an older server able to do this upgrades it before
attaching, so after installing a new
.B odin-seat
the next attach is enough.
.TP
.BI "mouse [\-s " name "] " event " " x " " y " [" button ]
Deliver a mouse event at desktop cell
.IR x , y
(1-based) from a script:
.BR press " (with an optional button number), " drag ", " release ", " wheelup " or " wheeldown .
.SH THE MENU KEY
Tap Alt (Option on a Mac, either side) and let go.
Then:
.TP
.B O
Open a new window, framed, on top.
.TP
.B C
Close the focused window (its program is hung up).
.TP
.B E
Split the focused window: then
.B R
opens on the right and
.B B
opens below; Esc cancels.
.TP
.B K
Force-kill the focused window's foreground job and shell after a
.BR y / n
confirmation. The question stays associated with that window if another
client changes focus. Unsaved work is lost.
.TP
.B Z
Zoom the focused window to the whole desktop, or back.
.TP
.B F
Screen redraw: repaint this terminal's text and images from scratch
when the display is garbled.
From a script,
.B odin-seat menu f
redraws every terminal attached to the session.
.TP
.B R
Rearrange: then
.B M
moves and
.B R
resizes with the arrows (Shift moves five), Enter finishes;
.B P
then
.B 1\-9
snaps the window to a ninth of the desktop;
an arrow alone sends it to that half of the desktop, left, right, top or
bottom;
.B H
hides it.
The four arrows on a window's frame, right of its title, do the same for
that window with a click.
.TP
.B X
Select the focused window, or unselect it.
A selected window fills the mark at the right of its frame, which a click
also toggles, and so does Tab on its row of the switch list.
The selected windows are the ones
.B L
lays out and
.B B
types to.
.TP
.B A
Select every window that shows; again, none.
.TP
.B L
Layout: arrange the selected windows together, or every window that
shows when none is selected.
Then
.B F
makes each the full desktop, one on another;
.B G
a grid, as near square on the screen as it allows, a short last row
sharing the width;
.B H
horizontal bars, each the full width;
.B V
vertical bars, each the full height;
.B 2
two columns with the windows shared between them.
The windows go in the order of their numbers and share the desktop
exactly, each program is told its new size, and Esc leaves them alone.
.TP
.B 1\-9
Focus that window and raise it.
The mouse selects any window.
.TP
.B M
Mark: copy mode on the focused window, see
.B COPY MODE
below.
.TP
.B T
Transfer: paste the mark (the last text copied, by copy mode or the
mouse) into the focused window, bracketed when the program asked for
bracketed paste.
.TP
.B H
Hide the focused window; the window beneath takes focus.
Its number, or the switch list, brings it back.
.TP
.B S
Switch window: a list of every window with its number, title, command
and size.
Type to filter by number, title or command; arrows move, Enter raises
the chosen window (unhiding it), Esc cancels.
.TP
.B B
Broadcast: while on, typing goes to several windows at once, each in
its own keyboard encoding.
With windows selected
.RB ( X ),
they are a group: what is typed in one of them goes to them all, and what
is typed in a window outside the group stays there.
With none selected it goes to every window in the session.
A status line says which.
.B B
again stops it.
.TP
.B U
The Unicode picker: an overlay of glyph categories driven by the arrows,
Tab and Enter, or the mouse.
The chosen character is typed into the focused window.
.TP
.B I
Choose an input method (Romaji, US-International, Pinyin, Zhuyin, Wubi)
that composes CJK and accented text the same way on every terminal.
.TP
.B ?
A help box with the keys, the mouse and the terminal setup.
Any key closes it.
.TP
.B D
Detach; the session and its programs keep running.
.TP
.B Q
Quit
.B odin-seat
after a
.BR y / n
confirmation, closing every window.
.PP
Esc, or a second tap, cancels the menu.
.SH COPY MODE
Menu
.B M
puts a cursor in the focused window that moves over its screen and its
scrollback; the window scrolls to keep it in view.
.TP
.BR h " " j " " k " " l ", arrows"
Move (Shift with an arrow moves five).
.TP
.BR 0 ", " $
Line start, line end.
.TP
.BR g ", " G
The oldest scrollback line, the last screen line.
.TP
"PageUp, PageDown, Ctrl-U, Ctrl-D"
Half a screen.
.TP
.BR v ", Space"
Start a selection at the cursor.
.TP
.BR / ", " ?
Search forward, backward: type the pattern, Enter.
The search wraps, and is case-insensitive unless the pattern has a
capital.
.BR n " and " N
repeat it forward and backward.
.TP
.BR y ", Enter"
Copy the selection (or the cursor's line when there is none) to the
system clipboard, keep it as the mark for
.BR T ,
and leave copy mode.
.TP
.BR q ", Esc"
Leave copy mode.
.SH MOUSE
Click a window to select and raise it.
Drag its frame to move it and its bottom-right corner to resize it.
Double-click a title bar to rename the window.
Drag inside a window to select text; releasing copies it to the system
clipboard.
The wheel scrolls the window under the pointer the way a native terminal
would: a program tracking the mouse gets the event, a pager on the
alternate screen gets arrow keys, and a shell prompt scrolls the
window's own scrollback (1000 lines).
Programs that track the mouse get their events in the window's own
coordinates, including motion for programs asking for it and pixel
positions for programs asking for those.
.SH TERMINALS
.B odin-seat
runs in any terminal.
It runs best in one that speaks the kitty keyboard protocol, reports
cell pixel sizes and shows images: Ghostty and kitty.
Elsewhere the Alt tap may not be reported (use
.BR Ctrl\-\e ),
and programs in windows are told images are not available.
On exit, and on a signal, the terminal is put back as it was found:
the screen from before is shown again, or on a terminal with no
alternate screen (mosh) the screen is cleared; mouse tracking, the
keyboard flags, the pointer shape and the cursor colour are reset.
.PP
On macOS Ghostty reports a bare Option tap only when Option is Alt.
Put
.IP
macos\-option\-as\-alt = true
.PP
in
.I ~/.config/ghostty/config
(or
.BR right ,
to keep left Option composing accented letters).
kitty needs nothing.
.PP
Images drawn by programs (kitty graphics or sixel) are shown in whatever
the terminal you attach from speaks: re-emitted as kitty graphics,
rasterised to sixel, or, on a terminal with neither, drawn as coloured
half-block cells, one pixel by two to the cell (see
.BR cell-pictures ).
Vector scenes and HDR pixels are offered to programs whatever is attached:
a terminal that cannot draw a scene is sent it drawn.
Programs that use Silkie's OSC 777 extensions are answered here too:
only the selectors and picture formats the attached terminals listed,
and that odin-seat can carry, are offered.
That includes Silkie's font selection, vector images and session fonts,
which reach a terminal attached later as well.
.PP
A file a program offers (kitty file transfer, or goblin-mosh's
download) is handed to the terminal when it speaks that protocol.
Otherwise a box asks whether to save it into your downloads directory;
nothing is saved, opened or run without a
.B y
typed at that box.
A drop onto a window lands in the window and cell it was dropped on.
See
.BR odin-seat-protocols (7).
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.I ~/.config/odin-seat/config
(or
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/odin-seat/config ,
or the file
.B ODIN_SEAT_CONFIG
names) holds
.I key = value
lines; a
.B #
starts a comment, values may be quoted, and a
.BI "[host """ name """]"
section applies only when the terminal you attach from names itself
that way in its XTVERSION reply (a case-insensitive substring, so
.B kitty
matches "kitty(0.48.2)").
An environment variable naming the same thing wins over the file; a
command-line option wins over both.
Problems are reported when
.B odin-seat
starts.
The keys:
.TP
.B shell
The program a new window runs; default the login shell.
.TP
.B scrollback
Lines of scrollback kept per window; default 1000.
.TP
.B download-dir
Where received files are saved (see
.BR ODIN_SEAT_DOWNLOAD_DIR ).
.TP
.B kitty-medium
.BR shm ", " tempfile ", " file " or " direct
(see
.BR ODIN_SEAT_KITTY_MEDIUM ).
.TP
.B term
The TERM given to programs in windows.
.TP
.B backdrop
The glyph the desktop backdrop is drawn with; default the light shade
block.
.TP
.B click-raises
.BR yes " (default) or " no :
whether a click raises the window it focuses.
.TP
.B cell-pictures
.BR yes " (default) or " no :
whether a terminal with neither kitty graphics nor sixel is drawn pictures
as coloured half-block cells.
With
.B no
such a terminal is told there are no pictures, and programs fall back to
text themselves.
.TP
.B mono
.BR yes " or " no :
strict black and white for this terminal, as
.BR \-m ;
useful in a host section for an e-paper terminal.
.TP
.B csi-limit
The maximum number of bytes in one CSI sequence
.RB ( ESC\ [
up to and including its final byte).
.B 0
(the default) means no limit. A longer sequence is ignored.
Cursor motion and editing that name a count still stop at the edge of
the screen.
The same value may be set with
.BR ODIN_SEAT_CSI_LIMIT ,
which wins over the file.
.TP
.B escape-limit
The maximum number of bytes in one OSC, APC or DCS sequence, not counting
its introducer and terminator.
.B 0
(the default) means no limit, so a vector image or any other payload is
kept whole. A longer sequence is discarded and not acted on.
The same value may be set with
.BR ODIN_SEAT_ESCAPE_LIMIT ,
which wins over the file.
.TP
.B menu-key
Which keys open the menu:
.BR alt ", " ctrl-backslash ,
both (the default, written as
.BR "alt, ctrl-backslash" ),
or
.BR none .
.PP
For example:
.IP
.nf
scrollback = 5000
backdrop = "."
[host "kindle"]
mono = yes
menu-key = ctrl-backslash
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_CONFIG
The configuration file to read instead of the default.
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_KITTY_MEDIUM
How images are handed to the terminal:
.BR shm " (the default), " tempfile ", " file " or " direct .
Use
.B direct
when the terminal is on the far end of a link where a local file or
shared memory would not be, such as a nested ssh session.
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_MONO
When set (and not 0), the same as
.BR \-m :
strict black and white for this terminal.
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_DOWNLOAD_DIR
Where received files are saved.
Otherwise
.BR $XDG_DOWNLOAD_DIR ,
the XDG user-dirs setting, or
.IR ~/Downloads ,
whichever exists, else
.BR $HOME .
Files are published without overwriting:
.IR report.pdf ", then " report.pdf.1 .
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_CSI_LIMIT
Maximum bytes of one CSI sequence. Overrides
.B csi-limit
in the configuration file.
.B 0
means no limit.
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_ESCAPE_LIMIT
Maximum bytes of one OSC, APC or DCS payload. Overrides
.B escape-limit
in the configuration file.
.B 0
means no limit.
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_TERM
The
.B TERM
given to programs in windows; the default is
.BR xterm\-256color .
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_TRACE
A file to which the client appends everything it paints to the
terminal, for telling a frame not shown from one never sent.
.TP
.B ODIN_SEAT_SOCK
Set in every window to the session's socket, so a
.B odin-seat
run inside a window acts on that desktop instead of nesting.
.TP
.B TMUX
When set, the client is inside tmux and wraps what it hands the outer
terminal (clipboard, images) in the tmux passthrough.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /tmp/odin-seat\- uid / name
The server's socket; one per
.BR \-L " name."
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/odin-seat
Input method dictionaries (set at build time by the install prefix).
.TP
.I ~/.config/odin-seat/config
The configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/odin-seat/sessions
Where
.B save
writes and
.B restore
reads by default.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a failed command or a refused attach.
A client that meets a server from an older build upgrades it in place
when that server can be upgraded (see
.BR upgrade );
one too old for that is refused with a message, and closing its windows
and running
.B odin-seat kill-server
replaces it.
A client older than the server is told to run the newer one.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR odin-seat-protocols (7),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR kitty (1)
